---
title: Page data
summary: $page.data (your load data, plus url / params / route / status / form / error) reads inside islands. A load that returns a promise streams into the island live.
related:
  - data-state/remote-functions
  - data-state/state-context
  - regions/client-islands
---

An island reads `$page.data`, and the rest of the page object of Kit, exactly as a normal Kit component does. Your `load` runs on the server as always. Its data reaches each island on the page.

```svelte title=Greeting.svelte
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
</script>

<p>Bonjour, {page.data.user.name} — you are on {page.url.pathname}</p>
```

All of the page object crosses the boundary: `data`, `params`, `url`, `route`, `status`, `form`, and `error`. It is the same data that your `load` returns, treated as client-visible: the same contract as a `csr = true` Kit page.

::: tip When to use
Use this when an island needs the own data of the page (the current user, the route params, a `form` action result), without a prop threaded down. For data that an island fetches *itself* (on demand, on a click), use a [remote function](/docs/data-state/remote-functions).
:::

## Streaming promises

Kit lets `load` return a **promise** at any level of `data`: you send the page now, and the value follows when it resolves. On a normal Kit page, the browser streams it in. A `csr = false` page has no page-level hydration. Thus the own stream of Kit is inert there. But an **island has a client**. Thus ogygia streams the promise into the island.

```ts title=+page.server.ts
export const load = () => ({
  country: 'FR',                                   // sent immediately
  slow: fetch('/api/recommendations').then((r) => r.json())  // a promise — streamed
});
```

```svelte title=Recs.svelte
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
</script>

{#await page.data.slow}
  <p>Loading recommendations…</p>
{:then recs}
  <ul>{#each recs as r}<li>{r.title}</li>{/each}</ul>
{/await}
```

The page shell, and the `Loading…` branch of the island, **paint immediately**. The first paint is never blocked on the slowest promise. Each promise resolves on its own as it settles (fast ones first). The `{#await}` of the island flips to its result live, with no extra request.

A promise that **rejects** streams as an error, and shows the `{:catch}` branch. A promise that resolves to a value that holds **more promises** streams those too, recursively: the same as Kit.

## Custom types

Types that you teach Kit through the [`transport`](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/hooks#Universal-hooks-transport) hook round-trip into islands as real instances, both in plain data and inside a streamed promise. This is not limited to built-in types like `Date` and `Map`.

```ts title=src/hooks.ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';

export class Money {
  constructor(public cents: number) {}
  get pretty() { return `$${(this.cents / 100).toFixed(2)}`; }
}

export const transport = {
  ...ogygia.transport,
  Money: { encode: (v) => v instanceof Money && [v.cents], decode: ([c]) => new Money(c) }
};
```

`page.data.price.pretty` then operates inside the island. The getter runs, because `decode` rebuilt the class, not a plain copy.

## Notes

- **A component shared with a `csr = true` page** reads the correct page object in both worlds. On an island page, the seed feeds it. On a Kit-hydrated page, ogygia threads the own live `page` of Kit through to it. To read `page.data` in a shared header/footer is safe in all locations. No guards are needed.
- **A `form` action result** (`page.form`) and `page.error` reach islands the same way. Thus an island can render the outcome of a `<form method="POST">` without a prop.
- **Client navigation** (the [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router)) cannot run streamed scripts in the middle of a swap. Thus, on those loads, a promise is awaited on the server, and handed to the island already settled. `page.data.x` is still a promise that you `{#await}`. Thus your island code is identical in both cases.
- **Treat load data as client-visible**, exactly as on a `csr = true` page: it is serialized into the HTML. Do not put secrets in `load` data that you would not ship to the browser.
- A **server island** (`render: 'deferred'`) renders in isolation on its own endpoint, and does not receive the page seed. Give it what it needs through its props, or a remote function.
